 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+## 2Q Eviction Mechanism
+
+The cache uses a two-queue (2Q) eviction policy to resist sequential-scan pollution while protecting sheets repeatedly used for comparison. Newly admitted previews enter a FIFO "in" queue (A1). If accessed again while still in A1, the entry moves to an LRU "main" queue (Am) on eviction from A1. Entries in Am that are accessed again remain in Am and move to its MRU position. When the cache exceeds 12 GiB, the worker first evicts from A1's tail, then from Am's LRU position if A1 is empty.
+
+The size split between A1 and Am is tunable. A1 receives 25% of the 12 GiB budget (3 GiB); Am receives 75% (9 GiB). This split protects comparison sheets (which move to Am after first reuse) from long screening runs that would otherwise cycle through A1 and evict them before comparison.
+
+All 2Q bookkeeping — the two queue structures, entry positions, and size accounting — remains private to the preview worker. The review API, render queue, and pending-response protocol are unchanged.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q with 25/75 split (A1: 3 GiB, Am: 9 GiB) | LRU: lowest bookkeeping cost, quick adaptation to project switches; Frequency-based with aging: better retention of repeatedly popular clips | 2Q protects comparison sheets (repeatedly accessed, moving to Am) from eviction during long sequential screenings (one-pass clips cycling through A1). Accepts extra bookkeeping (two queues, position tracking) and a tunable split parameter to prevent scan pollution. LRU would evict comparison sheets during screenings; frequency-based would add counter maintenance and lag project switches. |
